mising moves:\n1. **Play triple 7s (♠7 ♣7 ♦7)** – This is a strong mid-level triplet. It avoids wasting the bomb potential (since it\'s not a bomb), but puts pressure on the landlord to respond with a higher triplet or combination. It also opens space in my hand quickly.\n2. **Play triple_with_single (e.g., ♠7 ♣7 ♦7 + ♠3)** – Could help get rid of the low ♠3, but risks wasting a good triple just to shed a weak card.\n3. **Play a pair like KK or TT** – Safer, slower, keeps options open, but less impactful.\n4. **Hold back and pass?** – Not allowed here; this is a free play, so I must play something.\n\nBest strategic choice:  \nPlaying **triple 7s** is ideal because:\n- It uses a strong natural triple without overcommitting.\n- It forces the landlord to use a higher triplet (like Q, K, A, or 2) or a bomb if they don\'t have one.\n- It helps accelerate our side’s card reduction.\n- Holding onto the Black Joker for now is wise — save it to counter a bomb later.\n\nAdding an attached card would only help if we\'re trying to rush out, but since my hand isn’t extremely short, better to play clean combos and keep flexibility.\n\nReason:  \nPlaying the **triple 7s** exerts pressure on the landlord while preserving synergy with my remaining cards. It avoids prematurely attaching weak singles that could be played later in safer contexts. It also gives my teammate information — showing strength in mid-range triples — potentially guiding their play.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♠7", "♣7", "♦7"]}'}
